  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "**Fully** factor the following trinomial.\n\n$8{y}^{2}-36y+40=$\n{{field-1}}$($\n{{field-2}}$y-5)(y\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$",
  "worked_solution": "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n8{y}^{2}-36y+40\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $4.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2{y}^{2}-9y+10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $2{y}^{2}-9y+10$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $2\\times 10=20$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient (which is $-9$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the constant is positive, we need to consider the *negative* factor pairs of $20.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $20$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-20),\\,(-2,-10),\\,(-4,-5).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-4,-5)$ add to $-9:$ \n\n$$\n-4-5=-9\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-4y-5y,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-9y+10 & = \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-4y-5y+10 & = \\\\ (2{y}^{2}-4y)-(5y-10) & = \\\\ 2y(y-2)-5(y-2) & = \\\\ (2y-5)(y-2) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{y}^{2}-36y+40 & = \\\\ 4(2{y}^{2}-9y+10) & = \\\\ 4(2y-5)(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
